1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,304
Right now our prototype has no backend.

2
00:00:02,624 --> 00:00:05,721
Everything a user does is saved
in that one browser,

3
00:00:05,721 --> 00:00:09,771
on that one device, and the clinics
and prices it shows are made up.

4
00:00:10,091 --> 00:00:14,468
So a parent in China cannot see
the insurance card their child added

5
00:00:14,468 --> 00:00:17,365
in Boston:
there is nowhere to fetch it from.

6
00:00:17,685 --> 00:00:22,357
Accounts, family links and insurance
cards all need one shared place to live.

7
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:29,021
Late on Friday afternoon an agent wrote
a 223-line decision record proposing where.

8
00:00:29,341 --> 00:00:32,563
This episode is about how it chose,
and what it protects.

9
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:35,607
A backend has a few jobs.

10
00:00:35,927 --> 00:00:39,167
A server receives every request
and checks the rules:

11
00:00:39,167 --> 00:00:41,367
who is asking, and are they allowed.

12
00:00:41,687 --> 00:00:43,244
A database remembers.

13
00:00:43,564 --> 00:00:45,463
A login step proves who you are.

14
00:00:45,783 --> 00:00:49,269
And because the team set one hard rule
at Monday's meeting,

15
00:00:49,269 --> 00:00:52,222
that the product must run
on both Mac and Windows,

16
00:00:52,222 --> 00:00:55,767
everything goes into containers
that start with one command.

17
00:00:56,087 --> 00:01:00,548
The record fills each slot:
Hono, a small framework for the server,

18
00:01:00,548 --> 00:01:03,544
with Zod to check
the shape of every request;

19
00:01:03,544 --> 00:01:08,072
Postgres for the database;
Better Auth with emailed codes for login;

20
00:01:08,072 --> 00:01:11,468
and Docker Compose
to run the three boxes together.

21
00:01:12,817 --> 00:01:14,673
The first question was language.

22
00:01:14,917 --> 00:01:16,005
You read Python.

23
00:01:16,325 --> 00:01:18,138
Why isn't the backend in Python?

24
00:01:21,817 --> 00:01:26,749
Because browsers only run JavaScript,
and our page is written in TypeScript,

25
00:01:26,749 --> 00:01:29,540
which is JavaScript with type checks added.

26
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:31,451
The record makes it a rule:

27
00:01:31,451 --> 00:01:35,342
the front end is a fixed constraint,
so the back end adapts to it.

28
00:01:35,662 --> 00:01:40,380
Both prototypes, triage and insurance,
are already written in TypeScript,

29
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:42,190
in their open pull requests.

30
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:43,635
With one language,

31
00:01:43,635 --> 00:01:48,262
the server and the page share
a single description of every piece of data;

32
00:01:48,262 --> 00:01:50,097
rename a field on the server,

33
00:01:50,097 --> 00:01:53,326
and the page fails its check
before anyone runs it.

34
00:01:53,567 --> 00:01:58,020
Python's main advantage
would be its libraries for AI retrieval,

35
00:01:58,020 --> 00:02:01,778
but the triage proposal looks up prices,
phone numbers

36
00:02:01,778 --> 00:02:06,580
and opening hours in checked fact cards,
not by AI similarity search.

37
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:08,469
So Python stays an exit:

38
00:02:08,469 --> 00:02:11,871
a separate service later,
behind the TypeScript one.

39
00:02:12,191 --> 00:02:15,477
Express lost for having
no built-in request checks,

40
00:02:15,477 --> 00:02:17,282
NestJS for too much ceremony

41
00:02:17,282 --> 00:02:20,568
for three people,
and hosted backends like Supabase

42
00:02:20,568 --> 00:02:22,373
because user and health data

43
00:02:22,373 --> 00:02:27,146
would sit with a third party before the team
has settled its privacy rules.

44
00:02:27,466 --> 00:02:32,010
Fastify was just as good;
the record calls it an equal alternative.

45
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:35,099
For the database, Postgres.

46
00:02:35,419 --> 00:02:38,781
The data is about links:
a parent is linked to a student,

47
00:02:38,781 --> 00:02:41,435
and every permission check follows
that link.

48
00:02:41,755 --> 00:02:44,432
MongoDB lost for exactly that reason,

49
00:02:44,432 --> 00:02:49,713
and SQLite because a single file
doesn't suit many people online at once.

50
00:02:49,950 --> 00:02:52,126
One column is flexible on purpose:

51
00:02:52,126 --> 00:02:57,054
an insurance field stores not just a value
but how sure the card scanner was,

52
00:02:57,054 --> 00:03:00,510
where it came from,
and the raw text it was read from.

53
00:03:00,830 --> 00:03:05,457
The database we install also comes
with an add-on for searching by meaning,

54
00:03:05,457 --> 00:03:07,123
but nothing needs it today.

55
00:03:07,443 --> 00:03:09,470
It is an open door, not a plan.

56
00:03:09,790 --> 00:03:13,508
Changes to the tables travel
as numbered migration files,

57
00:03:13,508 --> 00:03:16,574
so every copy of the database
ends up the same.

58
00:03:17,917 --> 00:03:20,050
Login uses no passwords at all.

59
00:03:20,370 --> 00:03:23,435
You type your email
and receive a six-digit code,

60
00:03:23,435 --> 00:03:26,000
valid for five minutes, with three tries,

61
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,442
and the record requires the server
to keep only a scrambled hash of it,

62
00:03:30,442 --> 00:03:33,383
so the codes are never stored
as readable text,

63
00:03:33,383 --> 00:03:38,013
plus its own limit of one code a minute
per address, at most five an hour.

64
00:03:38,333 --> 00:03:42,024
The pull request notes you
confirmed email-only on Friday.

65
00:03:42,344 --> 00:03:46,371
The record's reasons:
every Chinese parent has an email address,

66
00:03:46,371 --> 00:03:48,637
and nobody has a password to forget.

67
00:03:48,884 --> 00:03:54,533
Text messages to Chinese numbers cost money,
WeChat login needs a registered company,

68
00:03:54,533 --> 00:03:59,252
and hosted login services keep user
data elsewhere and charge per user.

69
00:03:59,572 --> 00:04:02,665
Writing it from scratch
is how security holes happen.

70
00:04:02,985 --> 00:04:06,875
The library chosen,
Better Auth, runs inside our own server,

71
00:04:06,875 --> 00:04:09,534
and keeps everything in our own Postgres.

72
00:04:10,883 --> 00:04:14,040
Then the shape of the data,
the part that protects people.

73
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:18,219
Every account has a role, student or parent,
written once and,

74
00:04:18,219 --> 00:04:21,331
in this first version,
not changeable by the user;

75
00:04:21,331 --> 00:04:25,688
otherwise, the record notes, a student
could make themselves a parent.

76
00:04:25,933 --> 00:04:29,730
Who should be able to start a family link:
the student, or the parent?

77
00:04:32,433 --> 00:04:34,849
Only the student can start a family link,

78
00:04:34,849 --> 00:04:38,385
because the student is the patient
and owns the health data.

79
00:04:38,705 --> 00:04:42,173
They create an invite code:
at least eight characters,

80
00:04:42,173 --> 00:04:44,806
valid for seventy-two hours, usable once.

81
00:04:45,126 --> 00:04:49,102
Once linked, a parent sees
the student's profile and current

82
00:04:49,102 --> 00:04:52,017
insurance card, read-only, and nothing else;

83
00:04:52,017 --> 00:04:57,052
anything added later, such as triage chats,
is closed to parents by default.

84
00:04:57,372 --> 00:04:59,601
For now, card photos are not stored;

85
00:04:59,601 --> 00:05:03,686
the card is read on the device,
and only the text fields are kept.

86
00:05:05,033 --> 00:05:09,277
The first draft was read
by four reviewers from different angles:

87
00:05:09,277 --> 00:05:12,345
outside facts,
consistency with the repository,

88
00:05:12,345 --> 00:05:15,870
privacy and the data model,
and the issue's checklist.

89
00:05:16,190 --> 00:05:19,988
They raised thirty-one comments,
and a judge agent kept fourteen.

90
00:05:20,308 --> 00:05:24,419
The second version fixed,
among other things, three real mistakes:

91
00:05:24,419 --> 00:05:28,218
it had made up its own status and source
labels for insurance

92
00:05:28,218 --> 00:05:31,057
fields instead of reading
the insurance code,

93
00:05:31,057 --> 00:05:33,013
let linked parents enter cards,

94
00:05:33,013 --> 00:05:36,500
and planned to reuse services
that carry fictional data.

95
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:40,291
But the revision added a mistake of its own,
and Bugbot caught it.

96
00:05:40,611 --> 00:05:44,563
Unlinking only recorded the time
and left the link marked active,

97
00:05:44,563 --> 00:05:46,691
so a parent would have kept access.

98
00:05:47,011 --> 00:05:50,638
One missing word, in a written plan,
before any code existed.

99
00:05:50,958 --> 00:05:53,944
The fix landed twenty-one seconds
after the comment.

100
00:05:54,183 --> 00:05:56,892
If no bot had been watching,
what would have caught it?

101
00:06:02,683 --> 00:06:05,865
A test would,
and the record already asks for one:

102
00:06:05,865 --> 00:06:07,392
after a student unlinks,

103
00:06:07,392 --> 00:06:11,472
the parent's next request must be refused,
checked automatically.

104
00:06:11,792 --> 00:06:14,284
The decision itself is still a proposal;

105
00:06:14,284 --> 00:06:19,515
the issue asks the three of you to confirm
it in person before it is marked adopted.

106
00:06:19,750 --> 00:06:23,593
Still open: whether the servers
can be reached from China,

107
00:06:23,593 --> 00:06:28,032
which email service stays out
of QQ and one-six-three spam folders,

108
00:06:28,032 --> 00:06:32,934
whether parents may enter card details,
and how long deleted data lingers.

109
00:06:33,254 --> 00:06:36,373
Two product calls sit
in this technical document,

110
00:06:36,373 --> 00:06:41,401
both provisional until those issues decide:
parents see nothing new by default,

111
00:06:41,401 --> 00:06:44,710
and deleting a student's account
deletes their card.

112
00:06:45,030 --> 00:06:46,723
And one piece of housekeeping:

113
00:06:46,723 --> 00:06:49,318
two different decisions
are both numbered six.

114
00:06:51,867 --> 00:06:54,722
So: TypeScript, because the page decides;

115
00:06:54,722 --> 00:06:57,260
Postgres, because the data is links;

116
00:06:57,260 --> 00:07:00,503
emailed codes,
because every parent has email;

117
00:07:00,503 --> 00:07:03,707
containers,
because it must run on any laptop.

118
00:07:04,027 --> 00:07:07,099
And a data model whose real job is privacy.

119
00:07:07,334 --> 00:07:08,443
Three questions.

120
00:07:08,763 --> 00:07:10,577
Why isn't the backend in Python?

121
00:07:10,897 --> 00:07:13,478
What stops a student
from making themselves a parent?

122
00:07:13,798 --> 00:07:16,379
And what would have caught the unlink bug
without a bot?

123
00:07:21,617 --> 00:07:23,217
That ends this week's batch.

124
00:07:23,537 --> 00:07:26,972
Next week's episodes start
from next week's pull requests.

